---
import BaseLayout from '../../layouts/BaseLayout.astro';
import ArticleSchema from '../../components/ArticleSchema.astro';
import FaqSection from '../../components/FaqSection.astro';
import { site, description, pageTitle } from '../../content';
import { appBySlug } from '../../data/apps';

const app = appBySlug('justuno')!;
const path = '/apps/justuno';
const title = pageTitle(site, 'apps/justuno');
const desc = description(site, 'apps/justuno');

const faqs = [
  {
    "question": "Is Justuno available in the Shopify App Store?",
    "answer": "The listing checked on November 25, 2026 says the app is not currently available and directs support questions to Justuno. The company still markets list-growth products. Establish a supported Shopify installation path with Justuno before scheduling a rollout; listing unavailability alone does not prove the company or existing accounts have closed."
  },
  {
    "question": "Is Justuno an email marketing platform?",
    "answer": "Its current positioning centers on onsite list growth. Evaluate capture separately from lifecycle sending. A popup or quiz answer is useful only if the chosen sender receives the identity, permission and fields needed for its welcome journey."
  },
  {
    "question": "Can quizzes improve welcome relevance?",
    "answer": "A preference question can supply context for product education, but it does not establish a click or revenue uplift. Test a quiz against a simpler form with the same offer and eligible audience, and verify that answers arrive before the welcome message is selected."
  },
  {
    "question": "How much does Justuno cost for Shopify?",
    "answer": "Obtain a written quote for the supported installation, traffic definition, feature scope and integration work. The old dollar scenarios on this page were not verified quotes. Include the downstream sender and operating time in the comparison."
  },
  {
    "question": "Justuno or Privy for capture?",
    "answer": "Use the same capture fixture in each candidate: mobile display, suppression, permission and field delivery. A simple offer form may satisfy the requirement. A quiz warrants extra work when its answers can drive a defined product decision or welcome branch."
  },
  {
    "question": "Does a Shopify tag prove subscription permission?",
    "answer": "No. A preference tag describes a customer attribute; it is not a substitute for channel permission evidence. Keep the form wording, timestamp, source and subscription state separate from quiz answers."
  },
  {
    "question": "Which email sender should receive Justuno leads?",
    "answer": "Start with the sender you already operate. Demonstrate its supported connection and exact field mapping before selecting another tool. Sequenzy, Klaviyo and Omnisend review links are comparison paths, not evidence that every Justuno connection exists or supports the same fields."
  },
  {
    "question": "When should a store skip a quiz rollout?",
    "answer": "Defer when installation support is unresolved, answers have no downstream use, mobile friction outweighs the information collected, or no owner can maintain routing. Order count by itself is not a reason to buy or migrate."
  }
];
---
<BaseLayout title={title} description={desc} site={site} path={path}>
  <ArticleSchema title={title} description={desc} siteName={site.name} domain={site.domain} path={path} />
  <section class="container py-16">
    <a class="link" href="/apps">← All app reviews</a>
    <h1 class="hero-title mt-5">{app.name} review for Shopify stores</h1>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8">Justuno is a capture and onsite list-growth candidate for stores that need more context at signup. Its useful question is whether visitor preferences can reach an existing welcome journey accurately, rather than whether another popup can collect more addresses.</p>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8">Availability checked November 25, 2026: the <a href="https://apps.shopify.com/justuno-social-email-leads">Shopify listing</a> says the app is not currently available. <a href="https://www.justuno.com/">Justuno continues to market list-growth products</a>. Resolve supported installation with the company first; this is not evidence that every existing deployment stopped working.</p>
    <p class="mt-5 text-lg leading-8">This review contains proposed acceptance fixtures, not merchant test results or an independent ranking. No measured welcome uplift, subscriber gain or viral-week bill is asserted.</p>
  </section>
  <section class="container pb-12">
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Where capture adds useful information</h2>
      <p>A beauty store can ask which product texture a visitor prefers; an apparel store can ask which collection they want to hear about. Those answers can make the first email more relevant without pretending the quiz diagnoses a condition or guarantees a purchase. The benefit is information the shopper deliberately supplied.</p>
      <p>Justuno’s current site presents popups and visitor identification as list-growth products. Identification, a quiz answer and explicit subscription permission are different records. Do not turn an identified visitor into an opted-in subscriber merely because a profile match exists.</p>
      <p>A quiz and custom-field workflow should be demonstrated for the supported account and connector. Do not assume a historic integration carries every answer into Shopify and onward into any ESP. Ask for a sample payload and destination record rather than accepting a logo as proof of field coverage.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Capture identity and permission fixture</h2>
      <p>Use staff records for a new visitor, an existing subscriber, a previously unsubscribed customer and a customer changing their address. Record the storefront identity, Shopify customer ID where available, normalized email, source form and destination profile ID. Repeating submission must not create two welcome enrollments.</p>
      <p>Capture the displayed consent wording and version, timestamp, source URL and channel state alongside the preference answer. Submit the quiz without marketing consent and verify that the destination does not enroll that record. Then repeat with permission and inspect the resulting subscription event.</p>
      <p>An existing subscriber may update a preference without needing another welcome discount. An unsubscribed record must follow the destination’s supported resubscription process; overwriting a tag is not enough. Keep records with unresolved permission out of promotional sends while the owner investigates.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Quiz fields and event mapping</h2>
      <p>Proposed fixture: a three-answer product-preference quiz maps dry, light and rich texture choices to a documented enum field. Include an unknown or skipped answer path. Store question version separately so a later wording change does not silently reinterpret old profiles.</p>
      <p>Compare the raw capture payload with the Shopify record and sender profile. Check spelling, casing, list versus scalar type, missing values and update timestamps. A field named skin_type in one system and skin-type in another needs an explicit translation, not a hope that tags synchronize.</p>
      <p>Hold the welcome trigger until required fields arrive, or use a deliberate generic fallback. Simulate delayed delivery and a repeated webhook. Record the maximum acceptable delay for the actual journey and ensure retries do not send a second welcome. A weekly manual export cannot support a promise of immediate personalized welcome.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Four proposed storefront checks</h2>
      <p><strong>Product quiz:</strong> submit each answer on a product page, then inspect the selected welcome branch before sending to staff. Passing means the correct branch or documented fallback, exactly once. Click-rate lift is a separate experiment after routing works.</p>
      <p><strong>Cart-value offer:</strong> test carts below, at and above the proposed threshold; remove an item after opening the form. Verify discount eligibility in checkout, suppression after purchase and the behavior of a second browser session. Do not assume an exit-intent signal behaves identically on mobile.</p>
      <p><strong>Collection landing page:</strong> open a denim campaign URL with and without UTM parameters. Confirm the capture-source field survives redirects and does not overwrite an existing preference with an empty value. Check that a collection-specific promise leads to matching email content.</p>
      <p><strong>Recommendation surface:</strong> if offered in the account, test an out-of-stock item, an empty cart and a slow mobile connection. Require a usable page when the widget fails. Attribute any basket change to a controlled comparison, not to the presence of the module.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Pricing without invented merchant invoices</h2>
      <p>Ask what the supplier bills: sessions, pageviews, identified visitors, contacts or another unit. Confirm included usage, overage behavior, exclusions, minimum term, support and whether the requested quiz or connector is included. The current unavailable app listing is not a price quote.</p>
      <p>Build three estimates using your own low, normal and peak traffic. Include theme work, consent review, integration maintenance, experimentation time and the sender’s marginal cost. Ask whether disabling a widget changes billable usage; do not assume hiding it caps charges.</p>
      <p>Compare an existing simple form with the complete proposed stack. A lower capture rate may still produce better contribution if it avoids discounts, duplicate profiles and maintenance. A larger list alone does not pay a software invoice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Illustrative contribution calculation</h2>
      <p>This is arithmetic for a proposed experiment, not a Justuno result or quote. Assume 40,000 eligible sessions, capture at 3% versus 2%, and an equal 4% purchase rate among newly permissioned subscribers: 1,200 minus 800 gives 400 additional subscribers and 16 expected additional orders.</p>
      <p>At an assumed $60 order value and 50% contribution before capture costs, those orders contribute $480. If added software costs $100 and operating time costs $150, $230 remains before incremental discounts, returns and sender charges. These inputs must be replaced with store records; the example does not predict a lift.</p>
      <p>Randomize comparable eligible traffic and retain the same offer when comparing form complexity. Report completion, permissioned unique signups, delivery failures, unsubscribes and purchase contribution. Choose the observation window and sample requirement from baseline variability instead of declaring 200 completions or 90 days universally sufficient.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">A staged rollout with an actual rollback</h2>
      <p>First resolve installation support, document the incumbent form and export its configuration where available. Preserve consent evidence and existing suppression rules. Keep the prior form ready to restore; disabling an incumbent widget should not delete its records.</p>
      <p>Next run only staff traffic through the quiz and destination journey. Verify keyboard navigation, small-screen layout, validation errors, close controls and page behavior without the script. Confirm the new capture surface does not overlap a cart drawer or another popup.</p>
      <p>Only then expose a limited eligible cohort. One capture owner per URL avoids conflicting incentives. Pause the new widget if field delivery or permission state fails; pause the downstream welcome enrollment separately if already captured records are unsafe. Restoring the old form must not replay the new cohort into a second welcome.</p>
      <p>Expand only after the assigned owner can change an offer, suppress purchasers and trace one signup end to end. Store a mapping version, test record IDs and rollback steps in the runbook. The release gate is demonstrated behavior, not a prescribed order-volume threshold.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">How to compare siblings without forcing a new sender</h2>
      <p>Privy is the relevant sibling review when the job is a straightforward form or offer. Compare the same mobile and handoff fixture before paying for extra segmentation. Omnisend-versus-Privy covers the broader question of capture versus a combined messaging workflow; it does not prove that all quiz fields survive either route.</p>
      <p>For an existing Klaviyo or Omnisend account, first test whether its capture surface and custom properties already satisfy the brief. A Sequenzy review is useful only if lifecycle execution itself is under evaluation. Adding a second sender solely to justify a quiz creates another permission and queue boundary.</p>
      <p>The selection record should name the unmet capture requirement, supported installation, required fields, receiving journey and accountable operator. If no answer changes content or a product recommendation, a simpler form is a legitimate final choice.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="prose-shopify mb-12">
      <h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Weekly operation and useful stop signals</h2>
      <p>Reconcile completed forms against unique permissioned profiles and welcome enrollments. Inspect a sample of missing fields and delayed events rather than accepting a dashboard total. Track mobile abandonment and invalid addresses separately from completed quizzes.</p>
      <p>Review the bill against its actual metering unit and compare contribution with the simple-form cohort. Refresh questions when product choices change, preserving old values and mappings. There is no substantiated seasonal percentage decline or universal completion threshold in this review.</p>
      <p>Stop expansion when records cannot be traced, answers arrive after branch selection or incremental contribution does not cover the full operating cost. Preserve the useful preference data with its permission context where legitimately available; removal of the storefront widget is not automatic removal of destination profiles.</p>
    </div>
    <div class="card mb-12"><h2 class="font-serif text-3xl font-black">Compare the actual job</h2><ul>
      <li><a class="link" href="/apps/privy">Privy review</a> — compare a simpler capture brief</li>
      <li><a class="link" href="/compare/omnisend-vs-privy">Omnisend vs Privy</a> — capture versus a broader messaging workflow</li>
      <li><a class="link" href="/apps/sequenzy">Sequenzy review</a> — evaluate lifecycle sending separately</li>
      <li><a class="link" href="/use-cases/welcome-series">Welcome series</a> — define the receiving journey</li>
    </ul></div>
  </section>
  <FaqSection faqs={faqs} title="Justuno Shopify FAQ" />
</BaseLayout>
